      "side": 1,
      "message": "Could `p3s_perf_map_main()` get a short comment with the layout, and the reason from the commit message? It\u0027s the one place where a 4KB VMA maps a slice other than the one `vma_slice_offset()` gives, and why that\u0027s safe (a `VM_IO | VM_PFNMAP` VMA with no `-\u003eaccess`) is easy to miss later, so it would help to have it next to the code. Something like:\n\n```\n/*\n * p3s_perf_map_main - Map the main ring of @rb into a 4KB VMA\n * @rb: Ring buffer to map\n * @vma: 4KB VMA of the main ring, at vm_pgoff 0\n *\n *   vm_start\n *   ▼\n *   ┌──────┬──────┬──────┬──────┬──────┬──────┬─────\n *   │ U.0  │ D0.0 │ D0.1 │ D0.2 │ D0.3 │ D1.0 │ ...\n *   └──────┴──────┴──────┴──────┴──────┴──────┴─────\n *   U.0: slice 0 of the user page; DN.S: slice S of data page N\n *\n * The data starts at virtual slice 1 but at slice 0 of its kernel page,\n * so these PTEs don\u0027t follow vma_slice_offset(). That\u0027s fine because the\n * VMA is VM_IO | VM_PFNMAP and perf_mmap_vmops has no -\u003eaccess: GUP,\n * __access_remote_vm() and coredumps never read through them.\n */\n```\n\nWhile there, `PAGE_SIZE_KERNEL / PAGE_SIZE` is always 1 here, because `kernel/events/core.c` keeps the kernel `PAGE_SIZE`, and `offset_in_page()` and `data_offset / PAGE_SIZE` count kernel pages too. Would spelling the units out work for you? Then the index doesn\u0027t depend on the includer\u0027s `PAGE_SIZE`:\n\n```\n\t\tunsigned int slice \u003d (data_offset % PAGE_SIZE_KERNEL) / page_size;\n\n\t\t/* The data pages follow the one metadata page. */\n\t\tpage \u003d perf_mmap_to_page(rb, 1 + data_offset / PAGE_SIZE_KERNEL);\n```",
